# A negative contraction hides the negation in a two-letter suffix
# (`isn't`, `don't`), and a reader who skims past the `n't` reads the
# opposite claim. The full form gives `not` a word of its own.
#
# ProsePassive, ProseContrastiveNegation, ProseMinimizer,
# ProseDatedClaim, and ProseNotOnly keep their contracted forms, so
# `the value isn't written` draws a passive finding beside this one on
# the first pass. A curly apostrophe (`don’t`) does not match, because
# ProseTypography reports the curly quote first.
expr main [don't, doesn't, didn't, isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't, can't, couldn't, won't, wouldn't, shouldn't, mustn't, hasn't, haven't, hadn't, needn't]
let message "Negative contraction. Write not as its own word."
let description "Flags don't, isn't, can't, and the other negative contractions, which hide a negation in a suffix."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The cache does not expire."
allows "The client cannot retry."
